    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that formula makes sense. but the issue is that even when we have the math right, we still get these weird discrepancies where the system says we have stock but the shelf is empty, or we get sudden massive spikes in demand that we didn't see coming. it's like there's some invisible factor causing these sudden disruptions in our numbers. how do i account for those \"unseen\" variables in the safety stock calculation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To account for \"unseen\" variables, you need to introduce qualitative risk factors into your quantitative model. Consider increasing your Z-score to reflect higher perceived risk, or implement a tiered safety stock system where high-risk/high-variability items have inherently larger buffers. Furthermore, dedicate time to monitoring \"early warning indicators,\" such as unusually long supplier response times or sudden social media buzz about the component, to override the purely mathematical calculation.",
      "source": "target",
